# November 28, 2026: state hospitals and asylums of Illinois and Indiana, a batch of the themed pass on historic state
# hospitals. Peoria State Hospital (Bartonville) and the Indiana Medical History Museum (the Old Pathology Building on the
# Central State Hospital grounds) were already on the map and are not added again; the rest of the Central State grounds
# is entered here as a second place, with apart= saying why.
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia's articles on each institution and its list of Kirkbride Plan hospitals; the
# Park Service's National Register record for the Kankakee State Hospital Historic District; Asylum Projects' pages; the
# Kirkbride Buildings site and blog; the Indiana Archives' page on the state's other hospitals; the Indiana Family and Social
# Services Administration's pages for Logansport, Richmond and Madison State Hospitals; the Indiana Historical Bureau's
# Central State Hospital marker; the Kankakee County Museum; the Allen County genealogy pages and the History Center for
# Fort Wayne; the Indiana National Guard's Muscatatuck history and DVIDS news; Indiana University's disability history
# site; and news reports from WAND, NPR Illinois, the Lincoln Daily News, Shaw Local, the Associated Press and others.
# No patient is named. Pins: OpenStreetMap features and Wikipedia's points, every one looked at on the aerial.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'asylum_ilin_twrp','label':'State hospitals and asylums, Illinois and Indiana','added':'2026-11-28 07:08'}
AP='https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/'
KBL=W('Kirkbride Plan')
PLACES=[
N('jacksonville-state-hospital-il','Jacksonville State Hospital (Illinois State Hospital for the Insane)','Jacksonville','il','inst','gone',39.7195,-90.2345,'area','view','drive','','1851–2012',
  "Illinois's first state asylum. After Dorothea Dix lobbied for it, the legislature created the Illinois State Asylum and Hospital for the Insane on March 1, 1847; building began in 1848 and the first wards opened on November 3, 1851. The trustees chose the Kirkbride plan: a centre building five and a half storeys high with a wing for each sex stepping back on either side, and Asylum Projects says a trustee was sent to Indianapolis to copy the plans of the Indiana hospital. It was renamed the Illinois Central Hospital for the Insane in 1869 and Jacksonville State Hospital in 1910, turned to care of the developmentally disabled from 1974, and closed as the Jacksonville Developmental Center in November 2012. The Kirkbride building was pulled down in 1970, or in the 1970s by Wikipedia's account.",
  "The Kirkbride building is gone. The state still owned the 134-acre grounds at the end of 2012, and in a 2024 news report the city's mayor described 13 or 14 abandoned, boarded-up buildings there that could not be saved and needed to come down, after two large fires on the campus that autumn; the sources read do not say how old those buildings are.",
  '','State of Illinois land, abandoned and boarded up; the city has asked for money to pull buildings down. People walk past the buildings to a park next to the grounds. Look from the street: the empty buildings are unsafe and have burned.',
  [W('Jacksonville Developmental Center'),['Asylum Projects: Jacksonville State Hospital',AP+'Jacksonville_State_Hospital'],['Kirkbride Buildings: Jacksonville State Hospital','https://www.kirkbridebuildings.com/buildings/jacksonville'],['WAND: fire at former Jacksonville Developmental Center','https://www.wandtv.com/news/fire-at-former-jacksonville-developmental-center-sparks-frustration-from-city/article_2b0d54ba-b67f-11ef-ad3d-9be639ed36cb.html'],KBL],brick='?'),
N('anna-state-hospital-il','Anna State Hospital (Choate Mental Health and Developmental Center)','Anna','il','inst','standing',37.47894,-89.24231,'site','view','drive','','1869–',
  "The state's hospital for southern Illinois, authorized in 1869 with the one at Elgin and built on the Kirkbride plan; the north wing, finished first, took patients in March 1875. Fire destroyed one wing in 1881, which was rebuilt, and a second fire on January 3, 1895 destroyed a large part of the main building. In the 1950s and 1960s the wings were taken down and replaced by separate residential buildings. Since 1988 it has been the Clyde L. Choate Mental Health and Developmental Center, still a working state hospital.",
  "What is left of the Kirkbride building is the lower two storeys of the administration centre and a piece of wing on either side; the upper floors were lost to fire and taken off. The hospital's hilltop cemetery was used from 1872 to 1939, and most of its graves there have no marker.",
  '','A working state hospital run by the Illinois Department of Human Services, 1000 North Main Street. Look from the public road only.',
  [W('Anna State Hospital'),['Asylum Projects: Anna State Hospital',AP+'Anna_State_Hospital'],['Kirkbride Buildings blog: Anna State Hospital (2008)','https://kirkbridebuildings.com/blog/tag/Anna-State-Hospital'],['Cape Central High: Anna’s Choate State Hospital','https://www.capecentralhigh.com/?p=30304'],KBL],brick='?'),
N('kankakee-state-hospital-il','Kankakee State Hospital (Illinois Eastern Hospital for the Insane)','Kankakee','il','inst','standing',41.0999,-87.86313,'site','view','drive','','1877–',
  "The legislature created the Illinois Eastern Hospital for the Insane in 1877 and chose a site on the Kankakee River. Asylum Projects says it was begun as a Kirkbride building but changed during planning to a cottage plan, worked out largely from ideas of Frederick Howard Wines of the state charities board, with twenty-six large stone cottages; it became one of the best-known cottage-plan asylums. It opened in 1879 (Wikipedia gives September 4; the county museum and Asylum Projects say the first patients came in December). The South Infirmary burned in January 1885. It became Kankakee State Hospital in 1910 and, in 1974 or 1975 by different accounts, a centre for the developmentally disabled only, now the Samuel H. Shapiro Developmental Center.",
  "The old hospital grounds are the Kankakee State Hospital Historic District, 119 acres, on the National Register since August 4, 1995, in the Romanesque and Classical Revival styles. The Register record names James R. Willet and H. W. S. Cleveland; the Kankakee County Museum says James Lillie designed the hospital. The patients' cemetery, with 1,500 to 2,000 graves, is beside Kankakee Community College on land that was the hospital farm.",
  '','A working state developmental centre, 100 East Jeffery Street. Look from the public streets only.',
  [W('Kankakee State Hospital'),['National Register record: Kankakee State Hospital Historic District','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/95000987'],['Asylum Projects: Kankakee State Hospital',AP+'Kankakee_State_Hospital'],['Kankakee County Museum: Illinois Eastern Hospital for the Insane','https://www.kankakeecountymuseum.com/post/illinois-eastern-hospital-for-the-insane-kankakee-state-hospital-shapiro-developmental-nbsp-center'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='James R. Willet; H. W. S. Cleveland',archw='National Register record for listing 95000987 names "Willet, James R." and "Cleveland, H.W.S."; the Kankakee County Museum says "James Lillie designed the hospital"'),
N('elgin-state-hospital-il','Elgin State Hospital (Northern Illinois Hospital and Asylum for the Insane)','Elgin','il','inst','gone',42.01545,-88.28812,'area','view','drive','','1872–',
  "Authorized on April 16, 1869 for northern Illinois; Elgin sold bonds to buy 80 acres for it. Stephen V. Shipman, who had drawn the main building at Mendota in Wisconsin, designed the Kirkbride centre building, whose front ran just over 776 feet; it opened on April 3, 1872 with only the north wing finished. An annex followed in 1891, and by the mid-1950s the hospital held 7,700 patients. The annex was razed in 1972 and the centre building closed in 1973; after the Historic American Buildings Survey recorded it, it was pulled down in 1993 (Wikipedia; Asylum Projects says 1994). Most of the older buildings went between 1993 and 2008, and the place carries on as the Elgin Mental Health Center.",
  "The Kirkbride building and annex are gone. Wikipedia says that by 2018 only the powerhouse, still working, the 1967 administration building and some of the old roads, curbs and walks were left, with an old horse barn on the former farm; Asylum Projects names the 1916 powerhouse and a handful of 1930s E-shaped cottages. The hospital cemetery is kept up by volunteers and the City of Elgin.",
  '','A working state mental health centre, 750 South State Street. Look from the public road only.',
  [W('Elgin Mental Health Center'),['Asylum Projects: Elgin State Hospital',AP+'Elgin_State_Hospital'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Stephen Vaughn Shipman',archw='Wikipedia: "Shipman, who had designed the main building of the Mendota, Wisconsin State Hospital, was selected as the architect of the Elgin building"; Asylum Projects gives Stephen Vaughn Shipman'),
N('manteno-state-hospital-il','Manteno State Hospital','Manteno','il','inst','standing',41.23253,-87.80476,'area','view','drive','','1929–1985',
  "Authorized in 1927 and dedicated on November 21, 1929, a cottage-plan hospital whose first patients came in December 1930; Asylum Projects names the architects as William J. Lindstrom and Charles H. Hammond and calls the style Georgian. By 1936 it covered 1,200 acres with its own farm, fire department and power plant, and in 1954 it held 8,195 patients. It closed on December 31, 1985. The Illinois Veterans Home was dedicated in October 1986 in converted ward buildings at the north end, and more than 200 acres went to a non-profit group as the Illinois Diversatech Campus.",
  "Part of the hospital stands among later things: the veterans' home on the north end, factories and warehouses, housing and a golf course on the rest. The administration building was long a bank; in 2023 a non-profit was remaking it, with a vintage purse museum planned to open in 2024 (not confirmed here). Wikipedia says the abandoned Morgan Cottage is left, but a 2018 article says it was pulled down by May 2015. The hospital cemetery, east of the site, is now the State of Illinois Veterans Home Cemetery, with about 4,000 burials.",
  '','Mixed ownership: the State of Illinois veterans’ home (a working care home), businesses and private houses. Look from the public roads.',
  [W('Manteno State Hospital'),['Asylum Projects: Manteno State Hospital',AP+'Manteno_State_Hospital'],['Shaw Local: growing pains at Manteno State Hospital (2023)','https://www.shawlocal.com/daily-journal/news/local/2023/10/28/klasey-growing-pains-at-manteno-state-hospital/'],['Michael Kleen: Manteno State Hospital (2018)','https://michaelkleen.com/2018/03/27/mystery-and-madness-at-manteno-state-hospital/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='William J. Lindstrom; Charles H. Hammond',archw='Asylum Projects, Manteno State Hospital: architects "William J. Lindstrom and Charles H. Hammond"'),
N('chicago-state-hospital-dunning-il','Chicago State Hospital and Cook County Insane Asylum site (Read Dunning Memorial Park)','Chicago','il','inst','gone',41.95568,-87.79123,'site','public','walk','','1851–1970',
  "Cook County bought land here in 1851 for a poorhouse and insane asylum, the place that became known as Dunning; a three-storey brick poorhouse was finished in 1854. A separate three-storey insane asylum went up in 1869 and 1870, with a fourth storey in 1873, and cottage wards followed in 1885. In 1912 the county sold the 320 acres to the State of Illinois, which made the asylum the Chicago State Hospital; a fire on December 26, 1923 killed fifteen people and destroyed two wings. The hospital closed in 1970, its work taken over by the Chicago-Read Mental Health Center, and its buildings were later pulled down and the land built over.",
  "Nothing of the asylum buildings stands. Read Dunning Memorial Park, dedicated in 2001, keeps three acres of the old county cemetery, where an estimated 38,000 poor and unclaimed people were buried, among them residents of the poorhouse and asylum. In the middle stands an 1884 dedication stone from the poor farm with eight historical markers round it, and seven concrete circles in the grass mark the groups buried there. The Chicago-Read Mental Health Center still works nearby, and part of the old grounds is the Dunning-Read Natural Area of the Chicago Park District.",
  '','A memorial park on part of the old county cemetery (Wikipedia, which does not say who owns it), open ground, reached from West Belle Plaine Avenue near North Neenah Avenue. Street parking.',
  [W('Read Dunning Memorial Park'),W('Cook County Poor Farm, Illinois'),W('Chicago-Read Mental Health Center')],brick='no',bcol='none'),
N('watertown-state-hospital-il','Watertown State Hospital (Illinois Western Hospital for the Insane)','East Moline','il','inst','standing',41.53441,-90.41855,'site','view','drive','','1895–1980',
  "Created by an act of May 22, 1895 as the Illinois Western Hospital for the Insane. Governor John P. Altgeld laid the cornerstone on September 5, 1896 and, by Asylum Projects' account, suggested the Gothic style of the main building, a modified Kirkbride plan nicknamed the Castle on the Mississippi. The front wards were ready on May 16, 1898, when 336 patients were moved from Jacksonville. A 1908 county history says the large buildings were fireproof, of brick or stone. It was Watertown State Hospital from about 1910, East Moline State Hospital from 1927 and the East Moline Mental Health Center from 1975, and since 1980 the grounds have been the East Moline Correctional Center.",
  "The grounds are a state prison. The sources read do not say which hospital buildings still stand or which the prison uses; the aerial shows older winged buildings among the newer ones. About 300 former patients are buried in Resthaven Cemetery.",
  '','East Moline Correctional Center, a working minimum-security prison of the Illinois Department of Corrections, 100 Hillcrest Road. Look from the public road only.',
  [['Asylum Projects: East Moline State Hospital','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/East_Moline_Correctional_Center'],['Illinois Genealogy: Illinois Western Hospital for the Insane','https://illinoisgenealogy.org/?p=284'],W('East Moline Correctional Center')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('lincoln-state-school-il','Lincoln State School (Illinois Asylum for Feeble-Minded Children)','Lincoln','il','inst','standing',40.14056,-89.37972,'site','view','drive','','1877–2002',
  "Begun in 1865 by board members of the state's school for the deaf, incorporated in 1871 as the Illinois Institution for the Education of Feeble-Minded Children, and moved to a permanent campus at Lincoln that opened in 1877 or 1878 by different accounts. It was laid out on the cottage plan, and a farm colony with ten ward buildings, a kitchen, dining room and powerhouse was added in 1929. It became notorious for overcrowding and abuse; photographs taken here by Jack Dykinga for the Chicago Sun-Times won the Pulitzer Prize for feature photography. As the Lincoln Developmental Center it was closed in 2002.",
  "The old campus has stood empty since 2002; the farm annex became a prison. In 2025 the state's Monarch Youth Center opened on part of the property. In September 2026 the mayor described the remaining buildings as overgrown with their roofs falling in, and the state had set money aside to clear the site, with no timeline; the sources read do not say which buildings date from the 1870s. The patients' cemetery is now part of the prison grounds.",
  '','State of Illinois land, vacant, with a youth detention centre on part of it. Look from the public road only.',
  [W('Lincoln Developmental Center'),['Asylum Projects: Lincoln State School',AP+'Lincoln_State_School'],['Lincoln Daily News: plans for the Lincoln Developmental Center property (2025)','https://archives.lincolndailynews.com/2025/Feb/22/NEWS/today_LDC.shtml'],['NPR Illinois: Lincoln mayor on state sites (2026)','https://www.nprillinois.org/illinois/2026-09-08/with-logan-correctional-center-set-to-close-lincoln-mayor-urges-state-to-not-leave-another-mess']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('dixon-state-school-il','Dixon State School (Illinois State Colony for Epileptics)','Dixon','il','inst','standing',41.87347,-89.47991,'site','view','drive','','1918–1983',
  "Authorized in 1899 as the Illinois State Colony for Epileptics, but not paid for until 1913. The colony opened north of Dixon on May 1, 1918, a cottage-plan campus that a 2009 history in the Dixon paper counts at 128 buildings on 400 acres, with 1,100 acres of farmland. From 1921 it took the developmentally disabled as well; it was renamed Dixon State Hospital and later Dixon State School, and held more than 5,200 people in the early 1960s. Columns by the Chicago journalist Jack Mabley in the 1950s exposed its conditions. In 1983 the grounds were made into the Dixon Correctional Center.",
  "The grounds are a working state prison; the sources read do not say which colony buildings it still uses, though the aerial shows the old campus's roads and many buildings. The smaller Mabley Developmental Center, opened in 1987, is named for the journalist.",
  '','Dixon Correctional Center, a medium-security prison of the Illinois Department of Corrections. Look from the public road only.',
  [W('Mabley Developmental Center'),W('Dixon Correctional Center'),['Asylum Projects: Dixon State School',AP+'Dixon_State_School'],['Shaw Local: prison has lived many lives (2009)','https://shawlocal.com/2009/03/28/prison-has-lived-many-lives/at3hee2']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('logansport-state-hospital-in','Logansport State Hospital (Northern Indiana Hospital for the Insane, Longcliff)','Logansport','in','inst','standing',40.74287,-86.39754,'site','ask','drive','','1888–',
  "A law of 1883 called for three new state hospitals, at Logansport, Evansville and Richmond. The state bought the Shanklin farm a mile south-west of Logansport on October 4, 1883 and Cass County gave more land, and the Northern Indiana Hospital for the Insane opened on July 1, 1888 with 309 patients. Asylum Projects says the first superintendent, Joseph G. Rogers, laid out the ward buildings in a line along the ridge either side of a three-storey Victorian administration building; an assembly hall, later the chapel, followed in 1893. It became Logansport State Hospital in 1927, known locally as Longcliff for the cliffs on its grounds, and is the state's oldest working psychiatric hospital.",
  "The original 1888 administration building still stands and has been in use since the hospital opened; the hospital's museum began moving into it from the old pathology building at the end of 2014. Most of the old ward buildings have been pulled down and replaced; the chapel is still used, by Wikipedia's account.",
  '','A working state hospital, 1098 South State Road 25. Asylum Projects says the museum shows groups by appointment; ask the hospital first. Otherwise look from the public road.',
  [W('Logansport State Hospital'),['Asylum Projects: Logansport State Hospital',AP+'Logansport_State_Hospital'],['Indiana FSSA: Logansport State Hospital history','https://www.in.gov/fssa/dmha/state-psychiatric-hospitals/logansport-state-hospital/history/'],['AP: state hospital museum moving to new location (2014)','https://m.washingtontimes.com/news/2014/dec/2/state-hospital-museum-moving-to-new-location/'],['Discover Indiana: Longcliff Museum','https://discoverindianahistory.org/items/show/198']],
  brick='?'),
N('richmond-state-hospital-in','Richmond State Hospital (Eastern Indiana Hospital for the Insane, Easthaven)','Richmond','in','inst','standing',39.83866,-84.92607,'site','ask','drive','','1884–',
  "The land was bought in 1878 and building began in 1884, but the hospital was not finished and furnished until 1890; meanwhile the School for Feeble-Minded Youth used some of the buildings from 1887 to 1890. It opened formally on July 29, 1890 as the Eastern Indiana Hospital for the Insane. The first plan had nineteen buildings over about forty acres: an administration building with east and west wings and a rear centre, ten cottages, and a boiler house, laundry and other works; Asylum Projects calls it a cottage plan whose centre recalls the Kirkbride plan. Locals called it Easthaven, though that was never its official name.",
  "Of the original buildings, the administration building, the three next to it and two others survive, by the hospital's own history; the older buildings have exterior cupolas, and inside, railings and stained glass. Newer treatment buildings were added in 1991 and 2001. A museum of the hospital's history, begun in 1990, is in an old farmhouse at Indiana Avenue and NW 13th Street.",
  '','A working state hospital, 498 NW 18th Street. The hospital offers campus and museum tours to groups by arrangement through its own page; no photographs in patient areas. Otherwise look from the public road.',
  [['Indiana FSSA: Richmond State Hospital history','https://www.in.gov/fssa/dmha/state-psychiatric-hospitals/richmond-state-hospital/history'],['Indiana FSSA: Richmond State Hospital campus tours and museum','https://www.in.gov/fssa/dmha/state-psychiatric-hospitals/richmond-state-hospital/campus-tours-museum/'],['Asylum Projects: Richmond State Hospital',AP+'Richmond_State_Hospital'],['Waynet: Richmond State Hospital (2007)','https://waynet.org/waynet/spotlight/2007/070129-statehospital.htm']],
  brick='?'),
N('fort-wayne-state-school-in','Fort Wayne State School site (Indiana School for Feeble-Minded Youth)','Fort Wayne','in','inst','gone',41.09885,-85.12968,'site','public','walk','','1888–2007',
  "Indiana's school for the feeble-minded began at Knightstown in 1879 and moved to Fort Wayne in 1890, to a 60-acre property on the Hicksville State Road (now State Boulevard) that was then in the country. Its main building went up in 1888 and 1889 to designs by John F. Wing and M. S. Mahurin, and the campus grew to an administration building, cottages, a school, an industrial arts building, a hospital and a gymnasium. It was renamed the Fort Wayne State School in 1931. From about 1960 residents moved to a new campus at Stellhorn and St. Joe Roads; the state gave the old grounds to the city in 1979, and the administration building was pulled down in 1982 to make Northside Park. The institution itself, later the Fort Wayne Developmental Center, closed in 2007.",
  "The old campus is Bob Arnold Northside Park. The parks department kept a stone archway as a memorial, and a column from the State School building stands on East State Boulevard with a new plaque dated July 27, 2020; the old plaque went to the History Center, which also holds an 1898 cornerstone of the school. The institution's cemetery, with a single stone for 1901 to 1967 and no marked graves, is at 4900 St. Joe Road by the Purdue Fort Wayne entrance.",
  '','A city park, open ground, with a parking lot off Bob Arnold Drive.',
  [['Allen County genealogy: Indiana School for Feeble-Minded Youth','https://acgsi.org/genweb/fort-wayne/indiana-school-feeble-minded-in-allen-county-indiana.html'],['Asylum Projects: Fort Wayne Developmental Center',AP+'Fort_Wayne_Developmental_Center'],['The History Center: cornerstone of the school','https://fwhistorycenter.org/virtual-exhibits/vex17/7AB7B6F2-64A5-4372-AF41-785450569907.htm'],['Indiana Archives: other Indiana hospitals','https://www.in.gov/iara/services-for-public/search-archives-holdings/central-state-hospital-collection-index/other-indiana-hospitals-for-the-mentally-ill-and-developmentally-disabled']],
  brick='?',th=[41.09793,-85.13103],thn='Northside Park lot, Bob Arnold Drive',arch='Wing & Mahurin',archw='Allen County genealogy page: "John F. Wing and M. S. Mahurin were the architects of the structure" (the main building, 1888 to 1889)'),
N('muscatatuck-state-school-in','Muscatatuck State School (Indiana Farm Colony for the Feeble-Minded)','Butlerville','in','inst','standing',39.0513,-85.5358,'area','ask','drive','','1920–2005',
  "Opened as the Indiana Farm Colony for the Feeble-Minded on 1,813 acres in rural Jennings County: the first residents came on March 30, 1920 and the governor declared it open on December 15, 1920 (a National Guard history gives 1919 as the start). It became Muscatatuck State School in 1941, when a new administration building, Building No. 12, had just been put up for $31,644, and was later the Muscatatuck State Developmental Center, once the county's largest employer. The state closed it in 2005 and the Indiana National Guard made the grounds the Muscatatuck Urban Training Center, a training town for soldiers and first responders.",
  "The state's survey counted 34 buildings here as historically and architecturally significant, the Muscatatuck State Hospital Historic District, and the Guard's plan for the training centre was not to pull the whole district down, though it said the work would change it. A museum of the institution's history, opened in 2011, is in Building 5208, the former Tyler Hall; in 2020 the Guard said its tours were by appointment.",
  '','A working military training installation of the Indiana National Guard, closed to the public. The museum is seen by appointment through the training centre. Otherwise look from the public road.',
  [W('Muscatatuck Urban Training Center'),['Indiana National Guard: Muscatatuck history','https://secure.in.gov/indiana-national-guard/files/AM_MUTC_history.pdf'],['DVIDS: Muscatatuck facility celebrates 100 years (2020)','https://www.dvidshub.net/news/printable/384777'],['DVIDS: museum opens at Muscatatuck (2011)','https://www.dvidshub.net/news/printable/68457'],['Indiana Disability History: Muscatatuck administration building','https://dishis.sitehost.iu.edu/dishis/items/show/330']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('central-state-hospital-in','Central State Hospital grounds (Indiana Hospital for the Insane)','Indianapolis','in','inst','standing',39.76788,-86.21113,'exact','view','walk','','1848–1994',
  "Indiana's first state asylum opened in November 1848 with five patients in a single brick building on more than 100 acres on Washington Street, west of downtown. It was renamed the Central Indiana Hospital for the Insane in 1889 and Central State Hospital after 1926, and grew to two great ornate buildings, one for men and one for women, with nearly 2,500 patients by 1928. The women's department of 1878, the Seven Steeples, was its Kirkbride building; it and the other old wards were pulled down in the 1970s. The hospital closed in 1994 and the city bought the grounds in 2003 for redevelopment.",
  "The 1938 administration building, which never housed patients, was bought with the 1886 powerhouse in late 2013 and made into student apartments, the Central State Mansion; a state historical marker stands there. A lawn covers the site of the Seven Steeples, and the 1895 pathology building, now the Indiana Medical History Museum, stands to the west. Wikipedia says in one place that about ten hospital buildings are left and in another that only three were by December 2022.",
  '','Private apartments and housing on the old grounds, 202 Steeples Boulevard. Look from the street; the medical history museum nearby takes visitors.',
  [W('Central State Hospital (Indiana)'),['Indiana Historical Bureau: Central State Hospital marker','https://www.in.gov/history/markers/4324.htm'],KBL],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',apart='indiana-medical-history-museum: the museum is the 1895 Pathology Building, a separate building about 400 m to the west; this entry is the rest of the hospital grounds, the 1938 administration building and the marker'),
N('madison-state-hospital-in','Madison State Hospital (Southeastern Hospital for the Insane, Cragmont)','Madison','in','inst','standing',38.74577,-85.40184,'site','view','drive','','1910–',
  "The Southeastern Indiana Hospital for the Insane took its first patients on August 23, 1910, on a 585-acre campus on the bluff above the Ohio River, nicknamed Cragmont. It was built on the cottage plan, with about thirty two-storey buildings at first. Asylum Projects says one of the few early structures to survive is the gatehouse, whose stone front part dates from the 1840s, before the hospital, with a rear part of 1926. In 2005 the hospital moved into a treatment building made of restored, renovated and new parts, among the first psychiatric buildings in the country with a LEED rating.",
  "A working state hospital for about 120 patients. The aerial shows a group of old cottages with red roofs round a long central building; the sources read do not date them. A small cemetery on the grounds has 300 to 400 graves, only 17 with names.",
  '','A working state hospital, 711 Green Road. Look from the public road only.',
  [['Asylum Projects: Madison State Hospital','https://www.asylumprojects.org/index.php/Madison_State_Hospital'],['Indiana FSSA: Madison State Hospital history','https://in.gov/fssa/dmha/state-psychiatric-hospitals/madison-state-hospital/history'],['Indiana Archives: other Indiana hospitals','https://www.in.gov/iara/services-for-public/search-archives-holdings/central-state-hospital-collection-index/other-indiana-hospitals-for-the-mentally-ill-and-developmentally-disabled']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
]
